# Firmware images never belong in this repository.
# A dump is one specific vehicle's calibration and adaptation data: not ours to publish,
# and useless to anybody else.  The tests build their own synthetic image instead.
#
# The last two rules are REDUNDANT with *.bin and stay anyway.  Everything this tool writes
# is a .bin, so the pattern already covers them -- but the negations that re-admit the stage
# blobs are exactly the kind of line a later edit widens, and a vehicle dump reaching a
# public repository is the one mistake here with no undo.
*.bin
!stages/*.bin
!src/openm74/stages/*.bin
m74_backup_*
m74_selftest_*
# A .part file is HALF a dump, which is worse rather than better: --oneshot keeps a short
# read as <out>.part and --stitch writes through one, so a cancelled read leaves real
# vehicle flash in the working directory under a name *.bin does not match.
*.part

# The GUI keeps its log beside itself, which is this directory when it runs from a checkout.
openm74_log.txt

# Build products, including the finished ones.  The downloads live in GitHub Releases, not
# in the tree: a binary committed here is ~10 MB added to history FOREVER on every rebuild,
# for a file that goes stale the moment the source moves -- and a stale binary next to fresh
# sources is worse than no binary, because whoever downloads it gets bugs that were fixed.
#
# The .sha256 files go with them, for the same reason one step removed: a hash identifies one
# specific build, PyInstaller builds are not reproducible, so a hash sitting in the tree
# always describes some historical binary rather than the source next to it.  It belongs in
# the release notes, beside the file it actually identifies.  PyInstaller's .spec is written
# into build/ (tools/build_exe.py passes --specpath), so it needs no rule of its own, and
# Python 3 keeps every .pyc inside __pycache__.
build/
dist/
__pycache__/

# uv, virtualenv, linter cache.  uv.lock IS committed: it is what makes a checkout
# reproducible.
.venv/
.ruff_cache/

# OS and editor litter
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
# AppleDouble sidecars.  macOS writes one per file when a tree crosses a filesystem that
# cannot hold its metadata -- a USB stick, a network share, a copy to another machine.  They
# are invisible in Finder, so they get committed by an unsuspecting `git add -A` and then
# show up as noise in every diff on every other platform.
._*

# Assembler intermediates, plus the diagnostic stubs: their bytes live in klinebsl.py
# and tools/build_stages.py checks the assembled output against THOSE, which is what the
# tool actually uploads.  A committed .bin here would be a third copy with nothing to keep
# it honest.  (diag-*.bin needs its own line because !stages/*.bin above re-admits them.)
*.p
*.lst
stages/diag-*.bin
